<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Nop.Web.SmokeTests - the ASP.NET Core host smoke check, created by task 7.7
    (Requirements 4.1, 4.3, 4.4, 4.5, 4.6, 1.4).

    WHY THIS PROJECT EXISTS
    =======================
    Task groups 2-7 were validated by COMPILATION ONLY. Apart from the 4 fixtures in
    src/Tests/Nop.Tests, no ported line of this migration had ever been EXECUTED before
    this project, and roughly 40 entries in .kiro/specs/dotnetmodernization/runtime-deferrals.md
    are recorded as RESOLVED on the strength of code inspection or of throwaway probes run
    against synthetic host applications. This project runs the real thing.

    It boots the REAL Nop.Web host through WebApplicationFactory<Nop.Web.Program>: the real
    Program.Main, builder.Environment.UseNopHostingEnvironment(...), the real
    AddNopFramework() service graph, the real Autofac container built by NopHostedEngine, the
    real middleware pipeline in the real order, and the real compiled Razor views. Requests go
    through TestServer rather than a socket, so no port is bound and nothing is left running.

    NON-GATING, deliberately. tasks.md marks 7.7 non-gating and the migration's completion
    criterion remains Clean_Compile (Requirement 3.3). A failure here is a finding to record,
    not a build break - which is why this project is NOT added to any gate command.

    TARGET FRAMEWORK / SDK
    ======================
    Microsoft.NET.Sdk, not Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web: this is a test assembly, not a web
    application. The ASP.NET Core surface it needs
    (Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http, .Mvc, .Builder, .Routing, .TestHost) arrives through the
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.App FrameworkReference that Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing adds
    implicitly, so no explicit FrameworkReference is declared here (restating it would fail
    with NETSDK1086, the same trap Nop.Web.csproj documents).

    TargetFramework, Nullable, ImplicitUsings and GenerateAssemblyInfo come from
    src/Directory.Build.props; net10.0 is restated so it is verifiable from this file
    (Requirement 1.2). There is no Properties/AssemblyInfo.cs - this is a new project, not a
    converted one, so there is no hand-kept file to preserve and nothing is lost by
    GenerateAssemblyInfo=false.

    PACKAGES
    ========
    Versions come from src/Directory.Packages.props (ManagePackageVersionsCentrally=true,
    CentralPackageVersionOverrideEnabled=false), so every item is intentionally version-less.
    The test-SDK trio matches the pattern task 4.5 established for src/Tests/Nop.Tests -
    NUnit is pinned at 3.14.0 there because NUnit 4 removed the classic Assert.AreEqual
    surface, and this project uses the same pin for consistency even though it is new code.

      Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing  WebApplicationFactory<T> + TestServer. The one new
                                        central pin task 7.7 added (10.0.12, aligned with the
                                        other 10.0.12 Microsoft pins).
      Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk            VSTest host - on .NET the runner is a package.
      NUnit / NUnit3TestAdapter         framework + VSTest adapter.

    No mocking package: this project mocks NOTHING on purpose. Every assertion runs against
    the real host, because a smoke check that substitutes a double for the thing under test
    proves nothing about it.

    PROJECT REFERENCES
    ==================
    Nop.Web is referenced so WebApplicationFactory can find its entry point and so the tests
    can name Nop.Web types (Program, NopHostedEngine). The four upstream projects arrive
    transitively; Nop.Core is ALSO declared directly because the tests bind Nop.Core types
    (CommonHelper, EngineContext, ContainerManager, NopConfig, PluginManager,
    DataSettingsHelper) independently of Nop.Web, and the migration's own rule is that direct
    usage means a direct reference.

    NOT referenced FOR COMPILATION: Nop.Admin. Task 7.7's scope boundary was explicit -
    nothing under Administration/ was touched, that is group 8. TASK 8.8 added a
    BUILD-ORDER-ONLY reference plus an explicit copy; see the long note on
    CopyNopAdminToSmokeTestOutput below for why it is deliberately not a compiling
    reference. No test names a Nop.Admin type; every admin assertion reaches the assembly
    reflectively, which is how the host itself reaches it.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>

    <!--
      Suppress the NUnit1032 analyzer only where it fires on the shared WebApplicationFactory
      field, which is disposed in OneTimeTearDown rather than per-test. Kept narrow and
      explicit rather than blanket-disabling analyzers.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NUnit1032</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />
    <PackageReference Include="NUnit" />
    <PackageReference Include="NUnit3TestAdapter" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Presentation\Nop.Web\Nop.Web.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Libraries\Nop.Core\Nop.Core.csproj" />

    <!--
      ===================================================================================
      TASK 8.8 - RUNTIME DEFERRAL 8.4-1. READ THE TARGET BELOW BEFORE CHANGING THIS.
      ===================================================================================
      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" is the whole point: this establishes BUILD ORDER only.
      It is NOT a compiling reference, no Nop.Admin assets flow into this project, and
      Nop.Admin does NOT appear in Nop.Web.SmokeTests.deps.json. The assembly is placed in
      the output directory by CopyNopAdminToSmokeTestOutput instead.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Presentation\Nop.Web\Administration\Nop.Admin.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      ExcludeAssets="all"
                      PrivateAssets="all" />

    <!--
      TASK 8.8 - runtime deferral 8.2-1. Build-order only, for the same reason and by the same
      mechanism as the Nop.Admin reference above, but with an ADDITIONAL requirement: this
      assembly must NOT reach the test output directory. If it did, WebAppTypeFinder would load
      it as an ordinary base-directory assembly and it would become an application part without
      ever going through PluginManager's shadow copy - i.e. the plugin path under test would be
      bypassed and PluginDiscoveryTests would pass for the wrong reason.
      PluginDiscoveryTests copies the built dll into <contentroot>/Plugins/ itself.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Nop.Plugin.SmokeProbe\Nop.Plugin.SmokeProbe.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      ExcludeAssets="all"
                      PrivateAssets="all" />

    <!--
      TASK 10.1-10.3 - the first three REAL migrated plugins. Build-order only, same three
      attributes and the same reason as Nop.Plugin.SmokeProbe above: if their assemblies reached
      this project's output directory, WebAppTypeFinder would load them as ordinary
      base-directory assemblies and they would become application parts WITHOUT going through
      PluginManager's Description.txt parse and shadow copy - i.e. the plugin path would be
      bypassed and PluginViewRenderTests would pass for the wrong reason.

      THEY NEED NO COPY TARGET, unlike Nop.Admin and Nop.Plugin.SmokeProbe. Each plugin's
      OutputPath is 3.90's ..\..\Presentation\Nop.Web\Plugins\<ShortName>\, which IS the content
      root's Plugins directory that PluginManager scans - so building them puts them exactly
      where production puts them, and there is nothing to arrange. src/Presentation/Nop.Web/
      Plugins/* is gitignored.

      CONSEQUENCE, stated plainly: from this task on, every fixture in this suite boots a host
      with three real plugins present, because they are deployed rather than planted-and-removed.
      That is deliberate - it keeps the whole discovery path continuously exercised, as
      Nop.Plugin.SmokeProbe does - and no existing assertion counts plugins or plugin routes
      (checked). PluginDiscoveryTests still plants and removes its own probe plugin.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Plugins\Nop.Plugin.DiscountRules.CustomerRoles\Nop.Plugin.DiscountRules.CustomerRoles.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      ExcludeAssets="all"
                      PrivateAssets="all" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Plugins\Nop.Plugin.DiscountRules.HasOneProduct\Nop.Plugin.DiscountRules.HasOneProduct.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      ExcludeAssets="all"
                      PrivateAssets="all" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Plugins\Nop.Plugin.ExchangeRate.EcbExchange\Nop.Plugin.ExchangeRate.EcbExchange.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      ExcludeAssets="all"
                      PrivateAssets="all" />

    <!--
      TASKS 11.1 / 11.2 - the ExternalAuth and Feed plugins. Same three attributes and the same
      reason as the group 10 three above.

      THESE TWO ARE THE FIRST PLUGINS THAT SHIP STATIC ASSETS, and their OutputPath is what puts
      Plugins/<ShortName>/Content/** under the Nop.Web content root - which is the directory
      NopStaticFileProvider serves from. So PluginStaticAssetTests depends on this build-order
      reference for its premise, not just for discovery: without it the assets are simply absent
      and every "must serve" case would fail on its PREMISE BROKEN guard rather than silently
      passing.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Plugins\Nop.Plugin.ExternalAuth.Facebook\Nop.Plugin.ExternalAuth.Facebook.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      ExcludeAssets="all"
                      PrivateAssets="all" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Plugins\Nop.Plugin.Feed.GoogleShopping\Nop.Plugin.Feed.GoogleShopping.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      ExcludeAssets="all"
                      PrivateAssets="all" />
    <!--
      Tasks 12.1-12.5, the five Payments plugins. Same shape and same reasons as the three
      above. Note Payments.PayPalDirect additionally deploys a PRIVATE third-party assembly
      (PayPal.dll) into its plugin folder, which is what PaymentPluginTests asserts - and which
      the recipe alone did NOT do, because $(CopyLocalLockFileAssemblies) is false for a class
      library. See the long note in Nop.Plugin.Payments.PayPalDirect.csproj.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Plugins\Nop.Plugin.Payments.CheckMoneyOrder\Nop.Plugin.Payments.CheckMoneyOrder.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      ExcludeAssets="all"
                      PrivateAssets="all" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Plugins\Nop.Plugin.Payments.Manual\Nop.Plugin.Payments.Manual.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      ExcludeAssets="all"
                      PrivateAssets="all" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Plugins\Nop.Plugin.Payments.PayPalDirect\Nop.Plugin.Payments.PayPalDirect.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      ExcludeAssets="all"
                      PrivateAssets="all" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Plugins\Nop.Plugin.Payments.PayPalStandard\Nop.Plugin.Payments.PayPalStandard.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      ExcludeAssets="all"
                      PrivateAssets="all" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Plugins\Nop.Plugin.Payments.PurchaseOrder\Nop.Plugin.Payments.PurchaseOrder.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      ExcludeAssets="all"
                      PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ===================================================================================
    TASK 8.8 - RUNTIME DEFERRAL 8.4-1: getting Nop.Admin.dll into the directory the host
    actually scans. DO NOT SIMPLIFY THIS INTO A PLAIN ProjectReference.
    ===================================================================================
    THE DEFECT. Task 8.2 wired the Admin area end to end and Nop.Admin.csproj's
    CopyNopAdminToHostOutput target does drop Nop.Admin.dll into
    src/Presentation/Nop.Web/bin/$(Configuration)/$(TargetFramework)/ - verified present,
    5.8 MB. Yet Task_8_2_the_Admin_area_route_is_absent_until_Nop_Admin_compiles_KNOWN_GAP
    kept passing after Nop.Admin reached 0 errors. The reason is not the wiring:
    Nop.Core's WebAppTypeFinder scans AppDomain.CurrentDomain.BaseDirectory, and under
    `dotnet test` that is THIS project's output directory, not Nop.Web's. Nop.Admin.dll was
    never copied here, because this project references Nop.Web and Nop.Web deliberately does
    not reference Nop.Admin (design section 6). So the Admin area was absent from the test
    host, and every admin assertion deferral 8.3-2 asks for would have been vacuous.

    *** WHY NOT A PLAIN ProjectReference, WHICH ALSO PUTS THE DLL HERE? ***
    Because it would put Nop.Admin in this project's deps.json, and that changes WHICH
    MECHANISM is under test. On .NET the default AssemblyLoadContext binds from the
    deps.json-derived trusted-platform-assemblies list and does NOT probe the base
    directory - that is the root cause task 8.2 measured and fixed in
    AppDomainTypeFinder.LoadMatchingAssemblies (runtime-deferrals.md section 50). In
    PRODUCTION (`dotnet run` on Nop.Web) Nop.Admin.dll is present in the base directory and
    ABSENT from Nop.Web.deps.json - it is discovered by the path-based load. A plain
    ProjectReference here would exercise the TPA path instead, i.e. it would verify the
    admin area through a route production never takes, in exactly the respect that produced
    the defect. ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" + an explicit copy reproduces production.

    A MEASURABLE BONUS, and it closes an honest limit 8.2 recorded. 8.2's
    Task_8_2_WebAppTypeFinder_loads_base_directory_assemblies_without_throwing said of
    itself: "it only covers the non-regressing case ... reverting the fix would still make
    this test pass ... task 8.8, where Nop.Admin.dll really is such an assembly, is the
    first point at which this can be asserted for real." With this arrangement Nop.Admin.dll
    really IS such an assembly here, so that test is now a real assertion, and 8.8 asserts
    the deps.json absence explicitly so the property cannot silently decay into a plain
    reference later.

    BUILD-ORDER CONSEQUENCE, stated plainly. A ProjectReference of any kind makes MSBuild
    build Nop.Admin before this project, so `dotnet test src/Tests/Nop.Web.SmokeTests` now
    fails if Nop.Admin fails to compile. That is acceptable as of task 8.8 (Nop.Admin is at
    0 errors) and it is confined to this TEST project - the 7.6 gate on Nop.Web is
    untouched, because Nop.Web still has no reference to Nop.Admin in either direction.

    Nop.Admin's own dependencies are already here transitively through Nop.Web - verified:
    Autofac, AutoMapper, FluentValidation, Newtonsoft.Json, SixLabors.ImageSharp and
    System.ServiceModel.Syndication are all present in this project's output. Only
    Nop.Admin.dll itself was missing.

    GetTargetPath is used rather than a hardcoded bin path so the source location stays
    correct if Nop.Admin's output layout changes; it only reads the path, it does not build
    (the ProjectReference above already guaranteed the build).
  -->
  <Target Name="CopyNopAdminToSmokeTestOutput" AfterTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(NopAdminCopyToSmokeTestOutput)' != 'false'">
    <MSBuild Projects="..\..\Presentation\Nop.Web\Administration\Nop.Admin.csproj"
             Targets="GetTargetPath"
             Properties="Configuration=$(Configuration);TargetFramework=$(TargetFramework)">
      <Output TaskParameter="TargetOutputs" ItemName="NopAdminSmokeDrop" />
    </MSBuild>
    <ItemGroup>
      <NopAdminSmokeDrop Include="@(NopAdminSmokeDrop->'%(RootDir)%(Directory)%(Filename).pdb')"
                         Condition="Exists('%(RootDir)%(Directory)%(Filename).pdb')" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <Copy SourceFiles="@(NopAdminSmokeDrop)"
          DestinationFolder="$(OutDir)"
          SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />
    <Error Condition="!Exists('$(OutDir)Nop.Admin.dll')"
           Text="Nop.Admin.dll did not reach $(OutDir). Runtime deferral 8.4-1 has regressed: WebAppTypeFinder scans AppDomain.CurrentDomain.BaseDirectory, so without this file the Admin area is absent from the test host and every admin assertion in this suite is vacuous." />
  </Target>

</Project>
